n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand the constant coefficient is\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is odd and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is even, so $f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is odd. Also, $f(x)$ itself is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${a}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{0}=0.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{odd}$ and the $\\text{cosine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-340806/field-1" "q-340806/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "532a73e6-e026-57c9-9bad-57cd2f75b6df"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-340806"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-340798/field-1/f19e6df58422d7fb01cce9bdb84691d7edb0be968afd490c200de57c59561755" :answer/id #uuid "9162bdc4-e591-5e63-9485-2e7eb613cbbe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "odd" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340798/field-1/323c3707f259ac463f43078adb40da54bd6fbd62183256b458b52b49dc2a9da0" :answer/id #uuid "ffa92a51-84a2-540c-bca7-0304e5eba161" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "even" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340798/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "07bebc84-91ee-58eb-9f3b-f50945ed3afd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-340798/field-1/323c3707f259ac463f43078adb40da54bd6fbd62183256b458b52b49dc2a9da0" :answer-field/choices ["q-340798/field-1/f19e6df58422d7fb01cce9bdb84691d7edb0be968afd490c200de57c59561755" "q-340798/field-1/323c3707f259ac463f43078adb40da54bd6fbd62183256b458b52b49dc2a9da0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-340798/field-2/d1f6c915b4e3a1496843bec633b4d1faefcdef0d6c86e2f095279d4c052efb98" :answer/id #uuid "182378a8-ddaf-52ac-b8f9-76264aaf2592" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "cosine terms" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340798/field-2/ad5bfb9ccc3a6d948c3146ce91a4641b42b6fbd31026ea9a2acd2a98208ac3fa" :answer/id #uuid "24aa3ebe-af31-5609-a14e-9ce642314cb7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "sine terms" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340798/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "1a51923e-4489-556c-a0e4-e491550c7e0b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-340798/field-2/ad5bfb9ccc3a6d948c3146ce91a4641b42b6fbd31026ea9a2acd2a98208ac3fa" :answer-field/choices ["q-340798/field-2/d1f6c915b4e3a1496843bec633b4d1faefcdef0d6c86e2f095279d4c052efb98" "q-340798/field-2/ad5bfb9ccc3a6d948c3146ce91a4641b42b6fbd31026ea9a2acd2a98208ac3fa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-340798" :question/id #uuid "946adb9b-b2c0-577f-bc04-0222a7b9955a" :question/math-academy-id "q-340798" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={sin}^{2}⁡(x)+5$ on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Select the correct options below.\n$\\,$\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nConsequently, the {{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." :question/worked-solution "To check whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & ={sin}^{2}⁡(-x)+5 \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{2}+5 \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡(x)+5 \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=f(x),$ we have that $f$ is even.\n\nFor the Fourier series on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the sine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is even and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)$ is odd, so $f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)$ is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${b}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{even}$ and the $\\text{sine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-340798/field-1" "q-340798/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "532a73e6-e026-57c9-9bad-57cd2f75b6df"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-340798"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-338682"] :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Select the correct options below.$\\,$\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nConsequently, the \n{{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." :question/worked-solution "To determine whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=-f(x),$ we have that $f$ is odd.\n\nFor Fourier series on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand the constant coefficient is\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is odd and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is even, so $f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is odd. Also, $f(x)$ itself is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${a}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{0}=0.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{odd}$ and the $\\text{cosine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-340797/field-1/f19e6df58422d7fb01cce9bdb84691d7edb0be968afd490c200de57c59561755" :answer/id #uuid "d5784c40-de13-5fa6-b163-124f759274e2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "odd" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340797/field-1/323c3707f259ac463f43078adb40da54bd6fbd62183256b458b52b49dc2a9da0" :answer/id #uuid "ca682900-1eba-543a-b716-0b860948fc9e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "even" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340797/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b859716b-6b17-5b8a-8e68-720dff565b3a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-340797/field-1/323c3707f259ac463f43078adb40da54bd6fbd62183256b458b52b49dc2a9da0" :answer-field/choices ["q-340797/field-1/f19e6df58422d7fb01cce9bdb84691d7edb0be968afd490c200de57c59561755" "q-340797/field-1/323c3707f259ac463f43078adb40da54bd6fbd62183256b458b52b49dc2a9da0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-340797/field-2/d1f6c915b4e3a1496843bec633b4d1faefcdef0d6c86e2f095279d4c052efb98" :answer/id #uuid "98b66ab4-9a49-58d8-b944-2b46d501515e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "cosine terms" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340797/field-2/ad5bfb9ccc3a6d948c3146ce91a4641b42b6fbd31026ea9a2acd2a98208ac3fa" :answer/id #uuid "fd31a030-f578-5894-9cd3-f8c6ad46de0e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "sine terms" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340797/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "9a32e46f-5938-59af-bafa-76f422850638" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-340797/field-2/ad5bfb9ccc3a6d948c3146ce91a4641b42b6fbd31026ea9a2acd2a98208ac3fa" :answer-field/choices ["q-340797/field-2/d1f6c915b4e3a1496843bec633b4d1faefcdef0d6c86e2f095279d4c052efb98" "q-340797/field-2/ad5bfb9ccc3a6d948c3146ce91a4641b42b6fbd31026ea9a2acd2a98208ac3fa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-340797" :question/id #uuid "2741ca02-a0ca-568b-9994-9ebcd5878556" :question/math-academy-id "q-340797" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-{x}^{2}$ on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Select the correct options below.\n$\\,$\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nConsequently, the {{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." :question/worked-solution "To check whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x)-(-x)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=f(x),$ we have that $f$ is even.\n\nFor the Fourier series on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the sine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is even and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)$ is odd, so $f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)$ is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${b}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{even}$ and the $\\text{sine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-340797/field-1" "q-340797/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "532a73e6-e026-57c9-9bad-57cd2f75b6df"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-340797"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21803"] :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx) \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{{c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{c}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{d}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi$-periodic extensions of some functions from $x \\in [-\\pi,\\pi)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with the Fourier series shown above.  What are the Fourier constant coefficient, the $3\\textrm{rd}$ Fourier sine coefficient, and the $8\\textrm{th}$ Fourier cosine coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$?" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi$-periodic functions with Fourier series\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)), \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{{c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({c}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{d}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen for any constants $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$,\n\n$$\n\\alpha f(x) + \\beta g(x)\n\\sim\n\\frac{\\alpha a_0 + \\beta c_0}{2}\n+\n\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\n\\Big(\n(\\alpha a_n + \\beta c_n)\\cos(nx)\n+\n(\\alpha b_n + \\beta d_n)\\sin(nx)\n\\Big).\n$$\n\nUsing the linearity property of Fourier series, the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ are\n\n- $-3a_0+4c_0$ for the constant coefficient,\n\n- $-3a_n+4c_n,$ $n \\ge 1$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- $-3b_n+4d_n,$ $n \\ge 1$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore,\n\n- the Fourier constant coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ is $-3a_0+4c_0$,\n\n- the $3$rd Fourier sine coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ is $-3b_3+4d_3$, and\n\n- the $8$th Fourier cosine coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ is $-3a_8+4c_8$." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21805"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/238174bfcd0e07603a8516f9271efff876e36cf8021403be57b049ec31b60977.png)\nLet ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/a600da93d54ed8b4857befa58c1165e8c02b77d1c2fc5b1f460961dc135c50a7.png) be the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/1898332940848e9192873afe50ec9d9a005baf8bcb86ce77c66231321ba3fe0e.png)-periodic extension of a function from ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/5ff1e4e25630b889a7d37b5ac7252742b9b0f0abdf659ece1f3b0700815ddc44.png) to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/d33b55bb712e2f8437446299936998a79ee357a6aa77ce6c31932bb464868bb8.png) with the Fourier series shown above. What is the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/db700c8ee08453d9f4b0f347c4f88daf4186495494840905e9196b2243899653.png)th sine Fourier coefficient of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/c38b7489bfb362de743d5a53941eaddfc382f5d50756b13bc6a4fda893a692e9.png)" :question/worked-solution "The Fourier series of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/9363d37d53374519f1170d2c1c24ecb690a6810a304207ef21a0d1f3eba609de.png) is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/51c0039997e64ade9fb16a68ccaf87323ba205a85deb1020b5f74cd072dbf8f2.png)\n\nRecall that the corresponding Fourier coefficients of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/5c09997f1e5ab45e800dfc2a1fdbd766ce0e96033dd656a3128a5543bb6894a5.png) are\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/fdd27d69b84de936d93ebc06bc7e813d9002f40141d1d79fa787e46fc3dc4d23.png) for the constant coefficient,\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/6bd1cc889fa6814efed8288ad3a4be7c23fd86cd74c239ee933f770c87eb756d.png) ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/f582369f4b0075444e7e450e4a2b92413b8f5d55be2f27222637c1eb56cddd0f.png) for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/230850b2bfeae1c1cf561825fb543c43e08e105076be3f1241f5e4590f4bf537.png) ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/bbfce07e48b9a1eb79082aa046d29029cb6771b8243b5af1c0b9a353a5f1e379.png) for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore, the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/16cfd7f94cefe4ac0dca3045d59624a00c7f962d2a2cc1138a2407db521c8800.png)th sine coefficient of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/55bae3cc544df2d070b283a68deef7a5e53cf0f456aabd8062d0346a52662c03.png) is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/9264ca9ad64ff750e95f9523e56c79e665af874d3b13d601018061499c64ec41.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21804"] :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=x\\cos(2x)$ on $x \\in [-\\pi,\\pi].$ Complete the statements below by filling in the blanks.\nDetermine if the function $f(x)$ is odd or even, and which terms of the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." :question/worked-solution "To determine whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(-x)) \\\\  & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2x) \\\\  & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x) = -f(x),$ we have that $f$ is odd.\n\nFor Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi],$ the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\na_n=\\frac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\cos(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand the constant coefficient is\n\n$$\na_0=\\frac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is odd and $\\cos(nx)$ is even, so $f(x)\\cos(nx)$ is odd. Also, $f(x)$ itself is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi,\\pi]$ is $0.$ Thus, $a_n=0$ for all $n\\ge1,$ and $a_0=0.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{odd}}$ and the $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{cosine}}$ terms  in its Fourier series must be zero." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
